---
title: Divider
category: Components
description: Separate content with a horizontal or vertical line, with an optional label, using theui-svelte’s Divider component.
dir: ".lib/pages"
tags: [divider, separator, hr, rule, component, svelte, svelte-5, theui-svelte, component-library, ui, tailwindcss]
---

<script lang="ts">
  import DocLayout from "$lib/ui/doc/DocLayout.svelte";
  import DocHead from "$lib/ui/doc/Head.svelte";
  import DocBlock from "$lib/ui/doc/Block.svelte";
  import Code from "$lib/ui/doc/CodeBlock.svelte";
  import Example from "$lib/ui/doc/Example.svelte";
  import ConfigComponent from "$lib/ui/doc/ConfigComponent.svelte";
  import { processID, editURL } from "$lib";
  let { component }: {component: any } = $props();

  import { Divider } from "theui-svelte";
</script>

<DocLayout>
  <DocHead title="Divider" text="The Divider component draws a line between two pieces of content. It can run across or down, carry a label such as “OR”, and use a solid, dashed or dotted line." edit_url={editURL("divider")} />

  <DocBlock title="Example">
    <p class="not-prose">Import the component and drop it between two blocks. On its own it renders a real <code>&lt;hr&gt;</code>, so it means something to a screen reader as well as to the eye.</p>
    <Example>
      {#snippet example()}
        <div class="w-full">
          <p>Everything above the line.</p>
          <Divider />
          <p>Everything below the line.</p>
        </div>
      {/snippet}
      {#snippet code()}

```html
<script>
  import { Divider } from "theui-svelte";
</script>

<p>Everything above the line.</p>
<Divider />
<p>Everything below the line.</p>
```
      {/snippet}
    </Example>
  </DocBlock>

  <DocBlock title="Variants">
    <p class="not-prose">The <code>variant</code> prop sets the line style: <code>solid</code> (default), <code>dashed</code> or <code>dotted</code>.</p>
    <Example>
      {#snippet example()}
        <div class="w-full">
          <Divider />
          <Divider variant="dashed" />
          <Divider variant="dotted" />
        </div>
      {/snippet}
      {#snippet code()}

```html
<Divider />
<Divider variant="dashed" />
<Divider variant="dotted" />
```
      {/snippet}
    </Example>
  </DocBlock>

  <DocBlock title="Label">
    <p class="not-prose">Put content inside the component and it is placed in the middle of the line. This is the usual way to write an <b>OR</b> between two sign-in options.</p>
    <Example>
      {#snippet example()}
        <div class="w-full">
          <Divider>OR</Divider>
          <Divider variant="dashed">Continue below</Divider>
        </div>
      {/snippet}
      {#snippet code()}

```html
<Divider>OR</Divider>
<Divider variant="dashed">Continue below</Divider>
```
      {/snippet}
    </Example>
  </DocBlock>

  <DocBlock title="Label Alignment">
    <p class="not-prose">With a label, the <code>align</code> prop decides where it sits: <code>center</code> (default), <code>start</code> or <code>end</code>. The alignment follows the text direction, so it flips on a right to left page.</p>
    <Example>
      {#snippet example()}
        <div class="w-full">
          <Divider align="start">Start</Divider>
          <Divider align="center">Center</Divider>
          <Divider align="end">End</Divider>
        </div>
      {/snippet}
      {#snippet code()}

```html
<Divider align="start">Start</Divider>
<Divider align="center">Center</Divider>
<Divider align="end">End</Divider>
```
      {/snippet}
    </Example>
  </DocBlock>

  <DocBlock title="Vertical Divider">
    <p class="not-prose">Set <code>orientation="vertical"</code> to separate items that sit side by side. The divider takes the height of its parent, so place it in a flex row with a height.</p>
    <Example>
      {#snippet example()}
        <div class="flex h-16 items-center gap-4">
          <span>Profile</span>
          <Divider orientation="vertical" />
          <span>Settings</span>
          <Divider orientation="vertical" variant="dashed" />
          <span>Sign out</span>
        </div>
      {/snippet}
      {#snippet code()}

```html
<div class="flex h-16 items-center gap-4">
  <span>Profile</span>
  <Divider orientation="vertical" />
  <span>Settings</span>
  <Divider orientation="vertical" variant="dashed" />
  <span>Sign out</span>
</div>
```
      {/snippet}
    </Example>
  </DocBlock>

  <DocBlock title="Vertical Label">
    <p class="not-prose">A vertical divider takes a label too. The line is drawn above and below the text.</p>
    <Example>
      {#snippet example()}
        <div class="flex h-40 items-center gap-4">
          <span>Sign in</span>
          <Divider orientation="vertical">OR</Divider>
          <span>Create account</span>
        </div>
      {/snippet}
      {#snippet code()}

```html
<div class="flex h-40 items-center gap-4">
  <span>Sign in</span>
  <Divider orientation="vertical">OR</Divider>
  <span>Create account</span>
</div>
```
      {/snippet}
    </Example>
  </DocBlock>

  <DocBlock title="Customization">
    <p class="not-prose">Use <code>lineClasses</code> for the line and <code>labelClasses</code> for the text. The <code>class</code> prop goes on the element itself, which is where spacing belongs.</p>
    <Example>
      {#snippet example()}
        <div class="w-full">
          <Divider lineClasses="border-brand-500" />
          <Divider lineClasses="border-2 border-info-500" variant="dashed" />
          <Divider class="my-8" labelClasses="rounded-full bg-brand-500 px-3 py-1 text-xs font-semibold text-on-brand">NEW</Divider>
        </div>
      {/snippet}
      {#snippet code()}

```html
<Divider lineClasses="border-brand-500" />
<Divider lineClasses="border-2 border-info-500" variant="dashed" />
<Divider class="my-8" labelClasses="rounded-full bg-brand-500 px-3 py-1 text-xs font-semibold text-on-brand">
  NEW
</Divider>
```
      {/snippet}
    </Example>
  </DocBlock>

  <DocBlock title="Accessibility">
    <p class="not-prose">Without a label the component renders an <code>&lt;hr&gt;</code>, or a vertical separator with <code>aria-orientation="vertical"</code>, both of which assistive technology understands as a break between sections. With a label, the lines are hidden from screen readers and only the text is read, because a separator cannot hold a name of its own. Decoration alone does not need a divider: if the parts are already separate landmarks or headings, the line adds nothing.</p>
  </DocBlock>

  <DocBlock title="Configuration">
    <ConfigComponent {component} />
  </DocBlock>

